ation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Polymerization/condensation of formaldehyde and ammonia yields macromolecular precursors that, upon hydrolysis, release alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Established by heating formaldehyde and ammonia, with alanine detected mainly in the hydrolysate fraction. (Fox & Windsor, 1970, Science).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Parent body aqueous synthesis: formose + ammonia (surface to biochemical)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ca(OH)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"363-523K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"variable\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ca(OH)2\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Simulating parent-body/meteoritic aqueous alteration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formose and related aqueous chemistry in the presence of ammonia sets up macromolecular fraction releasing alanine upon hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Parent-body simulated aqueous alteration yields alanine precursors; alanine detected post-hydrolysis. (Kebukawa et al., 2017, Science Advances).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Exogenous DL-alanine: Interstellar ice photolysis and delivery\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_021\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"10-270K (formation), 300K (delivery)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"low/vacuum/1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"none\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"ultraviolet exposure; hydrolysis on warm-up\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photolysis and irradiation of ices generate alanine (racemic); subsequent delivery after melt/hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Lab simulation of ice photochemistry consistently yields racemic alanine, plausibly delivered by meteorites or cosmic dust. (Bernstein et al., 2002, Nature).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate transamination with glycine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_016\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Alum/KAl(SO4)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"alum (KAl(SO4)2)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 5, high glycine concentration\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate accepts amino group from glycine, producing alanine and glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Non-enzymatic, but alum-promoted transamination robustly demonstrated prebiotically. (Stubbs et al., 2020, Nature Chemistry).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate reductive amination with H2 (Ni catalyzed)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni (native, meteoritic, or mineral)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni metal (meteoritic or mineral)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral to alkaline pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"NH3/pyruvate imine intermediate is reduced by hydrogen activated on Ni surfaces.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"H2-driven alanine formation from pyruvate and NH3 proven catalytically; aligns with conditions in H2-rich alkaline vents. (Kaur et al., 2024, Chem).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination with Fe0 and hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe0 (native iron)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-343K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"mild\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native Fe0, consumed\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxylamine and Fe0 provide electrons and enable imine/oxime reduction, generating alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Distinct N\u2013O mediated entry, robust product detection but requires available hydroxylamine and native iron. (Muchowska et al., 2019, Nature).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"DL-alanine L-enrichment\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"variable\",\n        \"pressure\": \"variable\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"any plausible physical or chemical enrichment scheme\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Any plausible mechanism for L-excess\u2014symmetry breaking, crystallization, chiral surface adsorption, irradiation. No robust scenario yet established.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"No existing experiment provides a robust, prebiotically-justified L-alanine excess; L-enrichment is thus separated as a required, unsolved node. See Li et al., 2024 for D-enrichment on pyrite, but no L-alanine process established.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Shock synthesis from ammonium formate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_020\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"shock pressure/wave\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"variable\",\n        \"pressure\": \"extremely high (10\u201318 GPa)\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"impact-generated\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Extreme energy density from impact/shock wave drives C\u2013N coupling yielding amino acids.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mild alanine yield established under shock; dependent on local event frequency and precursor presence. (Suzuki et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_018\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of polymeric alanine precursor\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic hydrolysis or extended wet\u2013dry cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage releases alanine from macromolecular carbon or polymers.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Release of alanine from soluble/insoluble hydrolysable precursors observed in multiple systems; applies to formose/ammonia and parent-body chemistry. (Fox & Windsor, 1970; Kebukawa et al., 2017).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_019\",\n      \"name\": \"Photocatalytic pyruvate reductive amination (ZnS, illuminated)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ZnS mineral + UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"303-308K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ZnS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV/visible irradiation required\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"ZnS photoexcitation drives electron transfer, reducing pyruvate imine to alanine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Photoreduction on ZnS established, not feasible in dark hydrothermal interiors. (Wang et al., 2012, Chemical Communications).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal CO2/H2 \u2192 pyruvate \u2192 alanine (FeS promoter)\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2 is fixed to pyruvate on Ni3Fe