m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: L-Alanine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network synthesizes DL-alanine from diverse, literature-supported prebiotic routes, reflecting distinct geochemical settings and chemical mechanisms. Key hubs are the acetaldehyde/aminonitrile Strecker chemistry, pyruvate reductive amination, transamination, cyanosulfidic photochemistry, formaldehyde/amine condensation, Fischer\u2013Tropsch-type catalysis, impact/shock chemistry, and exogenous (meteoritic/ice) precursor hydrolysis. The network explicitly distinguishes between DL-alanine generation and a final L-enrichment step, in line with the literature consensus that abiotic synthesis intrinsically affords racemic alanine unless subsequent symmetry-breaking processes occur. Atom and charge balance is preserved in every reaction node, with parsable formulas for all molecular species including polymeric and precursor nodes. Each reaction is associated with its experimentally supported environment and mineral/chemical agent where relevant.\n\nMOLECULES (25 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CO2 (carbon dioxide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: H2 (hydrogen) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: C3H3O3- (pyruvate) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: CH3CHO (acetaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: HCN (h